# Delivery binaries are build outputs and are never tracked.
#
# A fixed filename does NOT make tracking them cheap: Git stores blobs, not
# names, so every rebuild adds a full new object to history regardless of what
# the file is called. Measured on two consecutive releases: the `.pyz` and the
# Windows `.zip` deltify well, but `waegis-linux-x64.tar.gz` does not compress
# against its predecessor at all (stream gzip - one early change shifts every
# following byte), so each release would add ~66 MB permanently. Committing a
# binary is also a one-way door: removing it later requires rewriting history.
#
# What IS tracked is the publication record under `waegis-<version>/`:
# RELEASE.md, SHA256SUMS, and release-manifest.json. The binaries are
# reproducible from a source checkout at that version:
#   uv run python 05_Software/waegis/packaging/build_release.py
#
# Builds land in `dist/` (already ignored by the repository root .gitignore);
# these patterns are the safety net for a build aimed somewhere else in here.
*.pyz
*.zip
*.tar.gz
